#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500281 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186500433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500281 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186500436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500281 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186500437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500281 #edb/ref 1085 "Mike needs to paint some walls. Each wall has an area of ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}$ square meters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500281 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "a171ca85-4730-530c-a9a0-53a782183f2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500281 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186500437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500288 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500288 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500288 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500288 #edb/ref 1071 "To complete his work, Mike has $4\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ liters of paint. Of this amount, he uses $3\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ liters. How much paint did Mike have left over when he finished? Express your answer as a mixed number in its lowest terms.\n\nMike had$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of paint left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500288 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17da2d52-c4a5-548d-8d46-a96ea5055bec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500288 #edb/ref 1074 "After finishing, Mike had $(4\\,\\frac{4}{5}-3\\,\\frac{3}{10})$ liters of paint left.\n\nWriting $4\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 5+4}{5}=\\frac{24}{5}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10+3}{10}=\\frac{33}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}-\\frac{33}{10}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $10.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{24}{5}$ over a denominator of $10,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}=\\frac{24\\times 2}{5\\times 2}=\\frac{48}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{10}-\\frac{33}{10}=\\frac{15}{10}\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{15÷5}{10÷5}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike had $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ liters of paint left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500292 #edb/ref 1071 "All the paint used on Mike's first day came from one can containing $3.78$ liters of paint. How much paint was left in the can after Mike's first day? Express your answer as a decimal.\n\nThere are\n{{field-1}}\nliters of paint were left over." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1da853e5-1005-5ae3-a2f4-0c314ac52689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500292 #edb/ref 1074 "From part 3, we know that Mike has used $2.82$ liters from the first can of paint.\n\nTo determine the amount of paint left in the first can, we need to calculate $3.78-2.82.$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.96$ liters of paint were left over." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500332 #edb/ref 1071 "During the first day of painting, Mike uses $1.5$ liters of paint in the morning and $1.32$ liters in the afternoon. How much paint did Mike use that day?\n\nExpressed as a decimal,  Mike used$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of paint." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d00a784-7b71-55f9-8814-6e64a549eeb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500332 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine how much paint Mike used, we need to calculate the sum $1.5+1.32.$\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $1.5$ and $2$ decimal places in $1.32.$\n\nTo add the numbers, they both need to have the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n1.5=1.50\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike used $2.82$ liters of paint." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500333 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500333 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500333 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500333 #edb/ref 1071 "To complete another job, Mike bought $4$ identical cans of paint, paying a total of $$95.64.$ Estimate the price of each can of paint by rounding the amount of money to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price of each can of paint is $\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500333 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a137dddd-e843-51d0-beae-620ba6ee763a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500333 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the price of each can of paint, we need to find an approximation for $95.64÷4.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n95.64\\to 96\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $96$ by $4$ as usual:\n\n$$\n96÷4=24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price of each can of paint is $$24.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500376 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction, the exact area of ​​each wall is$\\,${{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6918689-3a92-5cad-ac6b-0de2903bcad0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500376 #edb/ref 1074 "The exact area of  ​each wall is ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2} & ={{\\frac{17}{4}\\times \\frac{17}{4}}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\times 17}{4\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}=\\frac{289}{16}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500383 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500383 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500383 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500383 #edb/ref 1071 "To improve the paint's grip on the wall surface, Mike must dissolve $6.5$ grams of a particular additive in each liter of paint. How much additive is needed if Mike plans to use $2.5$ liters of paint?\n\nExpressed as a decimal, Mike must dissolve$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$grams of additive in $2.5$ liters of paint." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500383 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff821477-1720-563f-b73b-9b643857f797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500383 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the amount of additive that Mike must dissolve in $2.5$ liters of paint, we must multiply $6.5$ by $2.5.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $6.5$ and there is $1$ decimal place in $2.5.$\nTherefore, their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $1625$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n16\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nHence, $6.5\\times 2.5=16.25.$\n\nWe conclude that Mike must use $16.25$ grams of additive." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500414 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "764a4d3c-33b5-5db4-aa32-e4caa4192594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500414 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500418 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500418 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "826f2650-6a6f-51ad-a8b7-8bb2cdea681a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500418 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500428 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500428 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ccc0773-3284-528e-b8ba-8074d03aa841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500428 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500433 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500433 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a85a5177-b808-53a4-ad57-e81b42e9e3aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500433 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500434 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad27ff1f-ba19-5c5d-956f-6f0f53113fa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500434 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186